<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">
<policyconfig>

  <!--
    The Linux half of "the importer never attaches by itself".

    This is the mirror of org.artmoon.input-service.reconcile. ArtMoon's helper binds a device on
    the machine the device is PLUGGED INTO; this one attaches a device to the machine it ARRIVES on.
    Same shape, same reasoning, opposite end of the wire - and one asymmetry between them is real
    and deliberate: Windows needs no equivalent of either, because attach there works from a
    UAC-filtered limited token. Do not "fix" that by adding a service Windows does not need.

    The helper at /usr/libexec/artlight-input-service refuses to act without administrator rights,
    and on Linux the app has no way to hand it privilege except through pkexec. This action is what
    makes that pkexec call silent: allow_active grants it to the local active session with no
    authentication, so the one approval the user gives is the one that placed this file and the
    helper - not one per device.

    Placing this file is what requires the password. That is the moment to be thinking about what
    it grants: the active session may run exactly one program as root, and pkexec binds this action
    to it by the exec.path annotation below, so it cannot be pointed anywhere else.

    Why this is safe to grant, given the program takes input from an unprivileged process - and
    note that the input here is more dangerous than the exporter's, because it includes an address
    this machine will connect to:

      - It validates the exporter address with is_usable_exporter() and the busid with
        is_valid_busid(), BOTH linked in from the same policy the app uses. A second copy of those
        rules living in the helper would be a rule that drifts, and the drift would be on the side
        that runs as root.
      - The address charset admits only letters, digits, dot, colon, hyphen and underscore, and may
        not begin with a hyphen. That is enough for a hostname, an IPv4 literal and an IPv6 literal
        with port, and it cannot carry a shell metacharacter, a space or a newline.
      - It runs the client from an ABSOLUTE path out of a fixed list, never a bare name resolved
        through PATH, so what executes as root cannot be chosen by the environment it was handed.
      - It passes an argv vector straight to execv. There is no shell anywhere in this program, so
        nothing can be re-split on the way.
      - It keeps no state. It is told one action and performs that action; the kernel's own view is
        the only thing it consults.
      - It is statically linked, so there is no library for anything to inject into.
      - It sets its own PATH before parsing a single argument.

    Deliberately NOT allow_any or allow_inactive: a remote session, or a session nobody is sitting
    at, cannot use this. That distinction is the reason this is done with polkit rather than by
    making the helper setuid.
  -->
  <action id="org.artlight.input-service.attach">
    <description>Bring USB devices to this PC over the network</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to bring USB devices to this PC</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>yes</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/libexec/artlight-input-service</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
